INT. MINIVAN AT A TOLL PLAZA - DAY

Crumbs, juice boxes, a cracked sun visor. The windshield framed by a long line of brake lights.

DOLORES, 40s, drives with both hands. Beside her, a MAN in a ball cap fiddles with the map, face turned away. Behind them, a boy, WILLIE, 8, kicks the seat. A girl, MARISOL, 12, plugs one earbud in.

A stranger. She had never seen him before the gas station.

She grips the wheel tighter. He keeps studying the map. In the mirror, Willie sticks out his tongue. Marisol rolls her eyes and shushes him. They inch forward, one car length. The toll booth waits, a glass cage.

The stranger refolds the map. She watches his hands. He stuffs it in the glove box.

WILLIE
Mom, who is he?

DOLORES
Don't talk, Willie.

MARISOL
Mom, he's Uncle Frank.

DOLORES
Uncle Frank has a mustache.

MARISOL
He shaved.

The man in the ball cap turns at last. A broad smile, mustache missing, a pale stripe on his lip. He tips the cap.

FRANK
Surprise, kids.

WILLIE
Uncle Frank!

DOLORES
Frank, you scared me half to death.

FRANK
I had a plan.

DOLORES
It was a bad plan.

She laughs, tapping the wheel. He laughs. Marisol groans. The toll booth's arm lifts. Dolores hands the attendant exact change, rolls through, and merges onto the highway with a long exhale, while the boy throws his arms around Frank's seat.
